WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.403
Raft lets a majority replace a silent

00:00:02.503 --> 00:00:05.041
leader. At a forest fire, nobody votes.

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:09.120
In Raft, one leader accepts every write, and followers expect its

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:12.815
heartbeats. A write commits only when a majority stores it.

00:00:12.916 --> 00:00:16.393
Then the leader goes silent. A follower's timer expires,

00:00:16.493 --> 00:00:19.651
and the majority elects that follower. When the old

00:00:19.751 --> 00:00:23.931
leader returns, the majority's log replaces its uncommitted writes.

00:00:24.033 --> 00:00:27.140
Now take a forest fire. A commander sends orders to

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:31.352
five crews through vehicle computers and handhelds. In the valleys,

00:00:31.452 --> 00:00:34.685
the cellular link to the regional server often fails.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.364
Command belongs to a person. The organization names

00:00:38.464 --> 00:00:42.028
a successor, usually by voice radio. Devices accept

00:00:42.128 --> 00:00:45.046
orders offline, because crews cannot wait.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:48.848
Run the same failover at the fire. The commander's handheld

00:00:48.948 --> 00:00:52.711
goes silent, and the deputy's timer expires. But no majority

00:00:52.811 --> 00:00:55.866
votes, and crews already acted on the old orders.

00:00:55.966 --> 00:00:58.757
Crew 2 holds 'north flank' from the commander and

00:00:58.857 --> 00:01:02.001
'village edge' from the deputy. Timestamps sort the two

00:01:02.101 --> 00:01:04.656
orders, but they cannot say which one stands.

00:01:04.766 --> 00:01:08.695
Leases, fencing tokens and epochs exist. Each lets a quorum

00:01:08.795 --> 00:01:12.042
or a reachable server pick the holder. At a fire,

00:01:12.142 --> 00:01:15.866
the organization picks, and the server link fails first.

00:01:15.983 --> 00:01:18.694
While the commander is silent, no design gives

00:01:18.794 --> 00:01:22.054
all three: the commander records, command moves without

00:01:22.154 --> 00:01:24.438
them, and one device accepts at a time.

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:28.354
So succession is a policy choice. Keep any two, and pay for the

00:01:28.454 --> 00:01:32.940
third: two commanders, a commander who stops early, or a deputy who waits.

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:35.937
We separate a person's right to command from a

00:01:36.037 --> 00:01:39.769
device's right to write. Authority comes from signed grants

00:01:39.869 --> 00:01:43.211
and rules, fixed in advance; devices only enforce it.

00:01:43.316 --> 00:01:47.645
Every order shows one of four statuses: authoritative, provisional,

00:01:47.745 --> 00:01:51.082
residual, or in doubt. Residual means the person who

00:01:51.182 --> 00:01:54.651
gave it lacked command; it stays, labelled for review.

00:01:54.766 --> 00:01:57.250
A flip is the clearest cost: a crew acts

00:01:57.350 --> 00:02:00.609
on a provisional order, and it later turns residual.

00:02:00.709 --> 00:02:03.968
Policies differ widely in how many flips they cause.

00:02:04.083 --> 00:02:08.310
We compare six succession policies across three partition

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:11.499
cases and three states of the commander. A

00:02:11.599 --> 00:02:14.991
truth oracle checks 8,950 simulated incidents.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:18.799
First, our prototype's server lease costs most when nothing is

00:02:18.899 --> 00:02:22.783
wrong. The backhaul drops, the commander stays reachable over the

00:02:22.883 --> 00:02:26.093
mesh, yet nobody can record for over eighteen minutes.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:29.631
Second, a vehicle can relay an old heartbeat from the

00:02:29.731 --> 00:02:33.896
deputy and wake the commander's stopped device. Both then record

00:02:33.996 --> 00:02:37.428
for 107 seconds. An acknowledged timeout prevents it.

00:02:37.533 --> 00:02:40.874
Third, the duty chief signs a takeover while the scene

00:02:40.974 --> 00:02:44.188
has no link to the centre. The commander, who cannot

00:02:44.288 --> 00:02:47.374
know, gives 17 more orders, and crews act on them.

00:02:47.483 --> 00:02:51.741
If the takeover counts from its signature, all 17 turn residual:

00:02:51.841 --> 00:02:55.895
51 flips. If it counts from the deputy's acceptance, none do.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.612
Three steps remain: model checks of both results, a prototype

00:02:59.712 --> 00:03:03.324
on vehicle computers, and a walkthrough with rescue officers.

00:03:03.433 --> 00:03:05.615
A network can show who wrote an order.

00:03:05.715 --> 00:03:08.197
Only the organization can say who commands.
